A||N||4||mxbob||Smog problem||||5||
Z||000000||mxbob||04-27-2000||02:14 AM||||I’m having a problem with my ’86 Fiero V6 passing smog here in CA. The NOx (oxides of nitrogen) are to high until the smog inspector , using a vacuum pump pulls a larger vacuum on the EGR valve of approximately 10". The EGR valve , EGR solenoid , and all the vacuum lines have been replaced. Also I have a scan tool to check the computer with and the duty cycle on the EGR looks good as well TPS and MAP sensor readings. The vacuum pressure at the manifold is between 8" and 15" off idle but at the EGR valve I only get 2" to 3" the EGR valve is fully open at between 3" to 4" of vacuum . I can 10" plus vacuum with a hand pump at the manifold vacuum tube ( no restriction). This is a new EGR solenoid , does anyone know what the proper vacuum  this unit should hold is ? Any other ideas on what would cause high NOx would be appreciated .||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||04-27-2000||12:02 PM||||When my 88 4 cyl failed (just barely), the tech suggested that it was probably the EGR valve. Second choice was the catalyst. The EGR valve fixed the problem in my case, and also greatly reduced a "spark rattle" that I had at cruising speeds. <BR>Which got me thinking... (always a dangerous thing.) <BR>While I understand that all of your EGR pieces have been serviced, probably anything that will reduce combustion temps might be helpful. <BR>Is the timing set correctly? That will be waaaayyyyy easier than replacing a catalyst. Also might try Premium grade gas.<BR>No guarantees, just maybe worth a shot. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||04-27-2000||12:14 PM||||The EGR system is designed specifically to deal with NOx reduction.  The catalyst, on the other hand, is for reducing the hydrocarbon and carbonmonoxide emissions.  The catalyst might lower the Nox output a little but not significantly.  The EGR tube <BR>or the intake plenum where the EGR tube is connected may be restricted causing the EGR to have a limited affect when cycled.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Brian84SE||04-27-2000||12:39 PM||||The cat DOES reduce the NOx.  My car wouldnt pass emmissions (high Nox) even though the EGR worked fine.  I replaced the O2 sensor too.  After 6 times to the emission station, I changed the cat.  Low and behold.. the car sailed right through, without a problem.  It brought the Nox from 1800ppm down to approx. 400ppm.  It also reduced my HC too, but that was (barely) passing.  Read the service manuals- it will tell you all about the functions of a catalytic converter.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||04-27-2000||12:45 PM||||mshill... <BR>DOH!!! You're right. I had a brain-cramp, and forgot about the EGR tube completely. And that's the *first* thing I had to do to my Formula when I brought it home. Thanks.||||reg||1
